1
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:03,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it wrong to judge others?

2
00:00:03,762 --> 00:00:12,449
[SPEAKER_01]: And are we supposed to be aiming for that non-judgmental attitude towards everything?

3
00:00:12,469 --> 00:00:13,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's impossible.

4
00:00:14,791 --> 00:00:18,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Abstaining from a judgment is your judgment.

5
00:00:20,015 --> 00:00:21,016
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you're judging it.

6
00:00:23,798 --> 00:00:26,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is not whether you should not judge others.

7
00:00:27,201 --> 00:00:30,924
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is whether you should do it with or without ill will.

8
00:00:33,244 --> 00:00:34,245
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the problem is.

9
00:00:34,785 --> 00:00:37,227
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not that you judge and discriminate.

10
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:38,528
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the nature of mind is.

11
00:00:38,728 --> 00:00:40,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts are already forms of discrimination.

12
00:00:41,691 --> 00:00:42,952
[SPEAKER_00]: I want this, I don't want that.

13
00:00:44,593 --> 00:00:47,455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like whether you... whether they're fueled by ill-will.

14
00:00:49,397 --> 00:00:50,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you don't want.

15
00:00:51,178 --> 00:00:57,003
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're factually judging the behavior of some person, that is... that is a fact.

16
00:00:58,337 --> 00:01:14,200
[SPEAKER_00]: But if on account of that fact, you feel superior, start involving your conceit, you feel they're inferior, again start involving your conceit, you feel righteous, you feel ill-will, hatred, irritation towards them, that's why you're wrong.

17
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:20,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because there is a thought of judging and discriminating between agreeable, disagreeable, right or wrong.

18
00:01:22,142 --> 00:01:22,322
[SPEAKER_00]: But...

19
00:01:23,325 --> 00:01:31,208
[SPEAKER_00]: It's it's harder to differentiate problem on that level, because it's more personal and more kind of unpleasant as well, and also more ambiguous to work on.

20
00:01:31,568 --> 00:01:39,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Your conceits and, like, whatever, emotional background of it, it's much harder than to just...

21
00:01:39,311 --> 00:01:41,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I'll just remove the thoughts.

22
00:01:41,231 --> 00:01:42,332
[SPEAKER_00]: I just will not judge.

23
00:01:42,952 --> 00:01:45,713
[SPEAKER_00]: But that now makes you sacrifice your own intellect, your own mind.

24
00:01:46,393 --> 00:01:46,953
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then what?

25
00:01:47,034 --> 00:01:51,156
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't judge the clearly immoral, unwholesome, deluded behavior?

26
00:01:51,637 --> 00:01:57,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You pretend it's fine, because you didn't know how to deal with the problem of it that you were experiencing.

27
00:01:57,761 --> 00:02:01,363
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just a lazy attempt of trying to deal with the issue.

28
00:02:01,383 --> 00:02:05,706
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to recognize the mind judges.

29
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:07,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Yourself and others, that's what the mind does.

30
00:02:07,928 --> 00:02:09,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Picks and chooses, indiscriminates.

31
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:12,431
[SPEAKER_00]: But do you feel superior on account of it?

32
00:02:12,531 --> 00:02:13,232
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your problem.

33
00:02:13,272 --> 00:02:14,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't feel superior on account of your thoughts.

34
00:02:16,722 --> 00:02:19,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't feed them, don't entertain them, don't indulge them.

35
00:02:21,906 --> 00:02:23,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you feel ill-will on account of your thoughts?

36
00:02:23,807 --> 00:02:24,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

37
00:02:24,548 --> 00:02:26,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't welcome it, don't entertain it, don't indulge it.

38
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:28,190
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you just have thought.

39
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:34,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if that thought was fueled solely by your ill-will, once you stop fueling the ill-will, the thought will disappear.

40
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:46,413
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the thought was also factual recognition of the state of affairs, in terms of this person's behavior or that person's behavior, your ill-will will disappear, but the recognition of reality will not disappear.

41
00:02:46,493 --> 00:02:49,074
[SPEAKER_00]: And they are behaving like a foolish, immoral person.

42
00:02:49,434 --> 00:02:50,694
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't want to associate with them.

43
00:02:51,815 --> 00:02:52,795
[SPEAKER_00]: I wish them all the best.

44
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:53,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing...

45
00:02:53,975 --> 00:02:55,656
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have any ill-will or hatred.

46
00:02:56,823 --> 00:02:59,167
[SPEAKER_00]: or wishing harm and cruelty, as the Buddha would say.

47
00:02:59,627 --> 00:03:09,262
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you're a blinded fool now, that doesn't discriminate between different types of behavior, different types of people, different sets of values in the world.

48
00:03:10,218 --> 00:03:23,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the encouragement to really be able to define what is wholesome and unwholesome, and not to deny that those are factors that need to be discerned.

49
00:03:23,823 --> 00:03:24,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

50
00:03:24,983 --> 00:03:26,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Take responsibility for it.

51
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:32,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if something is wholesome or unwholesome, you are responsible in how you feel about it, so to speak.

52
00:03:32,746 --> 00:03:32,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

53
00:03:32,966 --> 00:03:34,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your feelings, they make you act.

54
00:03:35,127 --> 00:03:36,107
[SPEAKER_00]: They make you do stuff.

55
00:03:36,567 --> 00:03:42,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you restrained in regard to your feelings, or do you feel fully justified in regard to them?

56
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:54,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that kind of sense of righteousness, when people are, like, righteously indignant and full on ill-will, but they fully feel righteous and wholesome, because this behavior is wrong.

57
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:57,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but that doesn't justify the extent of your ill-will now.

58
00:03:58,215 --> 00:04:00,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That behavior is wrong in itself, full stop.

59
00:04:00,416 --> 00:04:01,056
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where it ends.

60
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:09,828
[SPEAKER_00]: But you feeling superior, having your will on account of it, that will never be justified by any behavior.

61
00:04:10,349 --> 00:04:18,219
[SPEAKER_00]: To the point, as the Buddha would say, even if highway robbers are sawing your limbs, one by one, extremely unjust behavior,

62
00:04:19,288 --> 00:04:20,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Killing you, basically.

63
00:04:20,689 --> 00:04:24,993
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have cruelty and ill-will towards them, you have not fulfilled the dhamma.

64
00:04:25,353 --> 00:04:27,995
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean, oh, it's fine for them to cut your limbs.

65
00:04:28,055 --> 00:04:28,476
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

66
00:04:28,836 --> 00:04:29,496
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unpleasant.

67
00:04:30,057 --> 00:04:32,759
[SPEAKER_00]: But you hating them is a completely separate thing.

68
00:04:32,779 --> 00:04:37,303
[SPEAKER_00]: The only person who is not free from ill-will, person who hasn't fulfilled the dhamma, will jump into.

69
00:04:38,744 --> 00:04:47,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, passion, ill-will, distraction, delusion is something you are always responsible, regardless of the circumstances that are pressuring, seemingly pressuring you into it.

